Date: Wed, 15 Dec 1993 23:34:22 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Reverse Polish Moon Treaty

I was at a meeting of the "Bay Area Reading Group" in September, 1992,
where variants of the Reverse Polish Moon Treaty were discussed. Mark
Miller and Robin Hanson were both there.

The "universal" interpretation was discussed at that time, wherein
each living person (no mention of iceheads) would get a share of the
_entire_ universe. I know that this was discussed, because the _units_
of the divvy were discussed, and I jokingly called them
"nanosteradians" (the sky is 4pi steradians, which gives each of
today's 6 billion people about 2 nanosteradians of the sky, and all
the stars, galaxies, etc., that lie in "their" claim).

There was also debate about the wisdom of either letting folks sell
their claims or _not_ letting them sell their claim. (I see big
problems either way.)

I think some of this discussion showed up here on the List, as I
recall offering to sell my claim--like my offer to sell my immortal
soul--for some pittance.

My point is not to take sides in the Krieger-Hanson debate, but to
note that the "universal" interpretation is the one I've heard debated
the most.

--Tim May

