Subject: Extropian children, birth

***************************************************************************

Jay Freeman says, regarding the kludge nature of childbirth:
> 
>  That's what I hear in my biology classes, too.  Much of the problem with
>the process of birth itself stems indirectly from human intelligence, and the
>large size of the brain at birth, hence the large size of the head, hence
>all sorts of mechanical difficulties at the time of labor.  

  In addition to the mechanical problems at birth (big head, small pelvis):
the same evolutionary pressure that made womens' hips wide, (rather than
deep) probably  _shortened_  the human gestation period. This may be one
reason why humans are relatively more helpless at birth than other
primates. This and no compelling evolutionary pressure not to make such
helpless babies. May make us more varied than we otherwise would be in life
too, birth changing the nature-to-nurture ratio. More time in womb leaves
more to nature. Earlier birth turns the job over to parents sooner.

 

