Subject: Extropian children, birth

Regardless of the value of minimally invasive childbirth, (and I find
LeBoyer's approach plausible, and I haven't heard that it leads to
difficulties), I can easily believe that even the most natural
and healthy childbirth is a big deal because of the envelope-pushing
features that have been described.

Any ideas about solutions? Bio-engineering for wider pelvises? Or
maybe just pelvises that widen at the end of pregnancy? How about
a kangaroo-style approach so that there can be even more development
outside of the mother's body? On the other hand, I haven't heard
of any really bright marsupials, so there may be a problem with
that one....

(LeBoyer is a doctor who decided that birth should be as smooth a
transition as possible (and medically safe--a lot of these 
procedures aren't followed if there are medical problems)--the
baby is born into a dim, warm room and given time to start breathing
on its own before the cord is cut. It's been quite a while since
LeBoyer births started--does anyone know of any follow-ups? I've
heard that all the Leboyer children were ambidextrous and none
of them were myopic, but I don't have any confirmation of this.)

