Date: Thu, 16 Dec 93 10:14:52 EST
From: eli@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: Glocks and other fine weaponry

I got a chance to try out the Glock 17 the other day and I have to say
that I like it a lot. I would be quite happy with one, I'm sure, but I
don't know yet which weapon I'll eventually want to buy.

(Did I mention that it can take two or three MONTHS to be able to
legally purchase a handgun in MA?)

While I remain a little concerned that all three of the Glock's safeties
are disengaged when you pull the trigger, that fact does have an upside
as well:

	* The Glock is easier to use and has an "intuitiveness factor"
	  similar to a revolver

	* Nothing to fumble with in a tense situation. If the gun is
	  loaded, it's good to go.

	* No DA vs. SA trigger pull differences. The gun has one
	  operating mode, which is one fewer operating mode to practice
	  than a more typical DA/SA weapon.

My accuracy with the weapon ranged from OK to non-existant. I
definitely did better out of the gate with the SIG P226 and the P220.
However, at my current level of proficiency (i.e. virtually nil) I'm
sure most of the problem is me and not the gun. I'm not sure how much
emphasis I should put on "what I shoot best with" when I don't shoot
very well at all yet.

I like the larger magazine (17 + 1) that the Glock 17 has, though I
wonder how much longer that will last. I like the lightness of the
weapon, though I could feel a slightly bigger recoil than the SIGs gave
me. Not unmanageable, but I wonder what it would be like to use a
hotter load in these weapons.  (Range loads being somewhat anemic in
comparison, I guess.)

Since I'll want a 9mm weapon first, I guess I'll end up trying to
decide between the Glock 17 and the SIG P226.  (Actually, had I the
money, I'd by the Glock 17 and the P220.) I've tried some Rugers, and
my friend's Smith & Wesson 9mm, but they just don't get it for me. The
Smith was nice, had a really smooth trigger pull, etc.  However, it was
pretty heavy and I found that kind of annoying.

Well, now that I'm officially a "gun nut," I'm all for more discussions
of such things here on the list, though I suppose that some people
would rather hear about bath tubs. How close to a "core value" is gun
ownership to the list? Somewhere in between bath tubs and nanotech, I
guess. Is this a discussion that should continue here, or should it
be "terminated with extreme prejudice?"

Elias Israel
eli@east.sun.com
