Subject: DREAMING:  Harv's guide to your inner Holodeck!

Emmanuel Mogenet <mgix@krainte.jpn.thomson-di.fr> asks:
>What about the time lost when you sleep ? Does anybody know about
>techniques, drugs, that allow you to shrink your necessary sleep time?

I have found a way to use my sleep time:  Lucid Dreaming.
Lucid Dreaming is where you realize you are dreaming, but don't wake up.  
At this point you can use the time in the dream for a consciously chosen 
purpose rather than just random dreaming.

You dream in cycles of about 90 minutes each.  Each cycle starts in
s-sleep (non-REM sleep/non-dream sleep) and ends with d-sleep (REM 
sleep/dream sleep).  The first cycle has about 5 minutes of dreaming at the 
end.  The second cycle has about 10 minutes, the third about 20 minutes,
the fourth about 40 minutes, and the fifth (usually final cycle) has almost 
an hour and a half of straight dream-time!  This is about 2-3 hours of 
dream time per night, depending on the individual.

Some uses of dream-time:
- Relaxation from a hard day.  I have worked on emergencies till midnight, 
taken a few hours dream-vacation at the beach, and been refreshed to go to 
work the next day.
- Problem solving.  I have gone to bed with a technical problem, worked it 
out during dream sleep, and actually woken up with the solution completely 
planned.  The solution actually was implemented on-the-job!
- Subconscious delving.  Ask a character in your dream about your 
subconscious fears, dreams, hopes, lost memories, explanations for unusual 
reactions or emotions, or anything else a therapist my try to wiggle out of 
your subconscious.  The dream character will speak clearly and "audibly" 
with answers straight from your own mind.
- Holodeck.  You can create anything in the dream.  Just like a holodeck, 
you plan what you want and program it in.  The mind is the best simulator 
yet!

It's cheap, it's free, it's already installed!
